## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of multi-tenant scheduling, latency guarantees, and dynamic resource control under pressure.

## Key Concepts
- Latency-aware scheduling across containers
- Per-container quotas and dynamic weights
- Runnable vs blocked time accounting
- Preemption policy and fairness under contention

## Code Example
```c
typedef struct {
  int id;
  uint64_t runnable_ns;
  uint64_t blocked_ns;
  uint64_t cpu_quota_ns;
  uint64_t base_weight;
  // ... other fields
} container_t;

static inline uint64_t total_time(container_t *c){
  return c->runnable_ns + c->blocked_ns;
}

void update_weight(container_t *c){
  uint64_t t = total_time(c);
  if (t == 0) t = 1;
  c->weight = (uint64_t)(c->base_weight * c->runnable_ns / t);
}
``` 

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you validate latency under mixed UI and batch workloads? 
- How would you adapt the policy for sudden memory pressure spikes or NUMA effects?

## Why This Is Asked
Stack growth is a core OS feature. This question tests understanding of page faults, per-thread state, synchronization, and safe growth under concurrent faults in a minimal kernel.

## Key Concepts
- Guard pages and dynamic stack growth
- Per-thread stack boundaries (stack_base, stack_limit)
- Atomic/locked updates to stack metadata
- Race conditions when multiple faults occur near the guard
- Basic testing: concurrent faults, growth cap, and resume correctness

## Code Example
```c
// Pseudo-skeleton: handle_stack_fault in a toy OS
void handle_stack_fault(Thread *t, void *fault_addr) {
  if (!in_stack_range(t, fault_addr)) return; // not stack fault
  acquire(&t->stack_lock);
  if (t->stack_limit - t->stack_base < GROW_SIZE) {
    release(&t->stack_lock);
    // terminate or signal OOM
    return;
  }
  map_pages(t, t->stack_limit - GROW_SIZE, GROW_SIZE); // grow downward
  t->stack_limit -= GROW_SIZE;
  release(&t->stack_lock);
  resume_execution(t);
}
```

